ch as an ⟦P55⟧ containing query results. For⟦P56⟧more, see ⟦P57⟧Freeing resources⟦PB1_58⟧.⟦P59⟧Locating and importing a database driver ⟦P60⟧⟦P61⟧You'll need a database driver that supports the ⟦P62⟧ you're using. To locate⟦P63⟧a driver for your database, see ⟦P64⟧SQLDrivers⟦PB1_65⟧.⟦P66⟧To make the driver available to your code, you import it as you would⟦P67⟧another Go package. Here's an example:⟦P68⟧Note that if you're not calling any functions directly from the driver⟦P69⟧package –- such as when it's being used implicitly by the ⟦P70⟧ package --⟦P71⟧you'll need to use a blank import, which prefixes the import path with an⟦P72⟧underscore:⟦P73⟧⟦P74⟧Note:⟦P75⟧ As a best practice, avoid using the database driver's own ⟦P76⟧⟦P77⟧for database operations. Instead, use functions in the ⟦P78⟧⟦P79⟧package. This will help keep your code loosely coupled with the ⟦P80⟧,⟦P81⟧making it easier to switch to a different ⟦P82⟧ if you need to.⟦P83⟧Opening a database handle ⟦P84⟧⟦P85⟧An ⟦P86⟧ database handle provides the ability to read from and write to a⟦P87⟧database, either individually or in a transaction.⟦P88⟧You can get a database handle by calling either ⟦P89⟧ (which takes a⟦P90⟧connection string) or ⟦P91⟧ (which takes a ⟦P92⟧). Both⟦P93⟧return a pointer to an ⟦P94⟧⟦P95⟧⟦PB1_96⟧.⟦P97⟧⟦P98⟧Note:⟦P99⟧ Be sure to keep your database credentials out of your Go source.⟦P100⟧For more, see ⟦P101⟧Storing database credentials⟦PB1_102⟧.⟦P103⟧Opening with a connection string ⟦P104⟧⟦P105⟧Use the ⟦P106⟧⟦P107⟧ function⟦PB1_108⟧ when you⟦P109⟧want to connect using a connection string. The format for the string will vary⟦P110⟧depending on the driver you're using.⟦P111⟧Here's an example for MySQL:⟦P112⟧However, you'll likely find that capturing connection properties in a more⟦P113⟧structured way gives you code that's more readable. The details will vary by⟦P114⟧driver.⟦P115⟧For example, you could replace the preceding example with the following, which⟦P116⟧uses the MySQL driver's ⟦P117⟧⟦P118⟧⟦